{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Variant picker
  Phase 8. Phase 2 §27.4, "Question 4 — How do I choose a variant?", built.

  Accepts:
  - product     {Object}  required.
  - section_id  {String}  required. Namespaces every id and radio group, so two
                          product sections on one page cannot collide.
  - swatch_shape {String} optional, 'circle' (default) or 'square'.

  Usage:
  {% render 'product-variant-picker', product: product, section_id: section.id %}

  THE PICKER IS BUILT FROM options_with_values, NOT FROM variants.
  Shopify's own performance guidance is explicit: product.variants truncates
  silently at 250 and costs render time on every request, while
  options_with_values is the object designed for this. Each product_option_value
  carries its own `selected` and `available`, so the server renders the correct
  initial state with no JavaScript involved.

  RADIOS, NOT A SELECT.
  Phase 2 §27.4: "Never a native <select> on a product page where the options
  are fewer than eight." A radio group also gives the selected state an
  accessible name change for free, which the same section requires — a checked
  radio announces itself, a styled <div> does not.

  UNAVAILABLE VALUES STAY IN THE DOM.
  §27.4 again: "Struck-through chip ... still focusable, with the state in its
  accessible name. Never removed from the DOM." So an unavailable value is not
  given the disabled attribute — that would take it out of the tab order and
  hide the fact that the option exists at all. It is struck through, its label
  says so in words, and the add-to-cart control is what refuses the purchase.

  WHAT `available` MEANS HERE.
  product_option_value.available is true when SOME variant carrying that value
  is available — not that the combination the customer has built is. The
  combination is resolved against the variant data the section publishes, by
  assets/product.js. Server-rendered state is therefore correct on load and
  refined on interaction, never the other way round.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- unless product.has_only_default_variant -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign shape = swatch_shape | default: 'circle'
  -%}
  <div class="variant-picker" data-variant-picker>
    {%- for option in product.options_with_values -%}
      {%- liquid
        comment
          A value renders as a swatch only when Shopify itself carries swatch
          data for it. Phase 1 DATA-03 recorded the prototype's three
          hard-coded hexes; nothing here invents a colour.
        endcomment
        assign has_swatches = false
        for value in option.values
          if value.swatch.color or value.swatch.image
            assign has_swatches = true
            break
          endif
        endfor

        assign group = section_id | append: '-option-' | append: option.position
      -%}
      <fieldset
        class="variant-picker__option{% if has_swatches %} variant-picker__option--swatch{% endif %}"
        data-option-position="{{ option.position }}"
      >
        <legend class="variant-picker__legend">
          <span class="variant-picker__legend-name">{{ option.name }}</span>
          {%- comment -%}
            The chosen value in words beside the option name. For a swatch row
            it is the only place the colourway is readable; for a text row it
            is redundant on screen but is what makes the fieldset's own
            announcement complete. It is updated by the picker's JavaScript.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <span class="variant-picker__legend-value" data-option-value-for="{{ option.position }}">
            {{- option.selected_value -}}
          </span>
        </legend>

        <div class="variant-picker__values">
          {%- for value in option.values -%}
            {%- liquid
              assign input_id = group | append: '-' | append: forloop.index
              assign unavailable = false
              unless value.available
                assign unavailable = true
              endunless
            -%}
            <input
              class="variant-picker__input visually-hidden"
              type="radio"
              id="{{ input_id }}"
              name="{{ group }}"
              value="{{ value.name | escape }}"
              data-option-position="{{ option.position }}"
              {% if value.selected %}checked{% endif %}
              {% if unavailable %}data-unavailable="true"{% endif %}
            >
            <label
              class="variant-picker__value{% if has_swatches %} variant-picker__value--swatch{% endif %}{% if unavailable %} variant-picker__value--unavailable{% endif %}"
              for="{{ input_id }}"
            >
              {%- if has_swatches -%}
                {%- if value.swatch.color -%}
                  <span
                    class="variant-picker__swatch variant-picker__swatch--{{ shape }}"
                    style="--swatch-fill: {{ value.swatch.color }};"
                    aria-hidden="true"
                  ></span>
                {%- elsif value.swatch.image -%}
                  <span
                    class="variant-picker__swatch variant-picker__swatch--{{ shape }} variant-picker__swatch--image"
                    style="--swatch-image: url({{ value.swatch.image | image_url: width: 80 }});"
                    aria-hidden="true"
                  ></span>
                {%- else -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    A mixed option: Shopify allows some values in an option to
                    carry swatch data and others not. The value still has to be
                    choosable, so it falls back to its name on screen.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  <span class="variant-picker__swatch variant-picker__swatch--{{ shape }} variant-picker__swatch--none" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                {%- endif -%}
                <span class="visually-hidden">{{ value.name }}</span>
              {%- else -%}
                <span class="variant-picker__value-text">{{ value.name }}</span>
              {%- endif -%}

              {%- comment -%}
                The same element the script manages, carrying the same hook.
                Without the attribute the script could not find this note, so
                it appended a second one the moment it ran and a screen reader
                heard "Small, Unavailable, Unavailable".
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- if unavailable -%}
                {{ ' ' }}<span class="visually-hidden" data-unavailable-note>{{ 'products.variants.unavailable' | t }}</span>
              {%- endif -%}
            </label>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </div>
      </fieldset>
    {%- endfor -%}
  </div>
{%- endunless -%}
